  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 35013268773694587a08292ed6fcfb4e, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 74426867712
    m_Localized: Temple History
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 488148819968
    m_Localized: Wat Khao Wong (Narai Cave) has been a <b>revered religious site</b>
      since the reign of <b>King Narai the Great (1656 - 1688)</b>, as King Narai
      passed through and <b>stayed at Narai Cave</b> on his journey to govern from
      Ayutthaya, leaving his residence at Narai Palace in Lopburi.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 880710508544
    m_Localized: "In the reign of <b>King Songtham (1610 - 1628)</b>, the king also
      visited to <b>worship the Buddha\u2019s footprint and stayed at this cave</b>,
      as the cave\u2019s climate remains comfortably cool year-round."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1036734423040
    m_Localized: "At Narai Cave, <b>ancient inscriptions</b> on the entrance wall
      reveal that it has been <b>a place of religious practice since the Anuradhapura
      period (over 1,200 years ago)</b>. Having experienced periods of prosperity
      and decline, the cave was revived as a Buddhist monastery <b>in line with Thailand\u2019s
      traditional governance and Buddhist heritage</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4689106805186560
    m_Localized: "Furthermore, historical records show that during the Sukhothai
      and Ayutthaya periods, <b>King Songtham sent a Thai delegation to Sri Lanka
      to worship the Buddha\u2019s footprint at Sumanakuta Hill</b>, and Sri Lankan
      monks shared that there was also a Buddha footprint at Thailand\u2019s Suwannabanphot
      Hill. <b>This confirms cultural exchanges in Buddhist traditions between the
      Lanka and Siam branches</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4688458831355904
    m_Localized: "At the entrance, <b>there are inscriptions in ancient \u2018Mon\u2019
      script (\u2018Pallava\u2019)</b>, used by South Indians. Evidence shows its
      presence in central and southern Thailand before the Sukhothai period, and
      <b>later, King Ramkhamhaeng the Great adapted this script, creating the foundation
      for Thai writing</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4688993881939968
    m_Localized: "Narai Cave\u2019s inscription, with <b>three lines of prose from
      the 7th century (\u2018Dvaravati\u2019 period)</b>, was translated by the Fine
      Arts Department:"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4689026685591552
    m_Localized: "<b>\u201CKantrajaya, founder of Anuradhapura, appointed Uncle Sinatha
      as his representative, along with the people of (Anuradhapura), to hold a celebratory
      dance for what is enshrined here.\u201D</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4689060504264704
    m_Localized: "This inscription suggests that <b>the local area was inhabited
      for centuries and might have been a prosperous settlement</b>. The name \u2018Anuradhapura\u2019
      reflects <b>ancient connections between the Lanka and \u2018Mon\u2019 peoples</b>,
      commemorated here in honor of shared heritage."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914118266544128
    m_Localized: "<b>This temple was granted permission for construction on July
      5, 1917, and received \u2018Visungkhama Seema\u2019 status on June 15, 1978</b>.
      The area covers 30 meters in width and 51 meters in length, totaling 1,500
      square meters. <b>The ordination boundary was consecrated on February 20, 1983,
      led by Somdet Phra Phutthacharn (Kiew Uppaseno) and Phra Thamma Sitthanaayok
      (Thongchai Sukhajano) of Wat Saket Rajaworamahavihara</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914122121109504
    m_Localized: Wat Khao Wong has been developed into <b>a model temple with outstanding
      achievements</b>, officially <b>registered as a historical site</b> by the
      Department of Fine Arts on December 4, 2002, as announced in the Royal Gazette,
      Volume 119, Special Issue 119 Ng.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914126562877440
    m_Localized: "Currently, Wat Khao Wong (Tham Narai) is located at 62/1, Moo 5,
      Khao Wong Subdistrict, Phra Phutthabat District, Saraburi Province. It <b>serves
      as a provincial meditation center in Saraburi</b>, practicing under <b>Phra
      Rajaprommayan (Luang Pho Ruesi Ling Dam)</b> with a focus on Maha Satipatthana
      Sutta meditation. The temple accepts meditation practitioners year-round, who
      must strictly adhere to the temple\u2019s regulations and traditions."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914133177294848
    m_Localized: "Additionally, Wat Khao Wong (Tham Narai) is a <b>habitat for rare
      flora and fauna</b>, such as Queen Moke (Wrightia sirikitiae D.J. Middleton
      & Santisuk), Chan Pha, Red-whiskered Bulbul, and albino squirrels, <b>preserved
      as part of Thailand\u2019s cultural heritage for future generations</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6941848672133120
    m_Localized: Advanced Level in Buddhist Theology, Honorary Master of Buddhist
      Studies (Vipassana Meditation)
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6945555350544384
    m_Localized: Current Position
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6945797777121280
    m_Localized: "\u2022 Abbot of Wat Khao Wong\n\u2022 Advisor to the Chief Monk
      of Saraburi Province"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6947897613475840
    m_Localized: Personal Information
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6947914722041856
    m_Localized: "\u2022 <b>Name</b>: Vacharachai Pussayanawin\n\u2022 <b>Date of
      Birth</b>: Sunday, July 19, 1942 (8th day of the waxing moon, Month 8, Year
      of the Horse)\n\u2022 <b>Father\u2019s Name</b>: Mr. Khong Pussayanawin\n\u2022
      <b>Mother\u2019s Name</b>: Mrs. Lamai Pussayanawin\n\u2022 47 Moo 4, Wang Daeng
      Subdistrict, Tha Ruea District, Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya Province"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6947990685081600
    m_Localized: Ordination
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6948895966879744
    m_Localized: "\u2022 <b>Date</b>: Thursday, April 29, 1982 (7th day of the waxing
      moon, Month 6, Year of the Dog)\n\u2022 <b>Location</b>: Wat Chantharam, Nam
      Suem Subdistrict, Mueang District, Uthai Thani Province\n\u2022 <b>Preceptor</b>:
      Phra Kru Uthaidham Koson, Wat Sangkhas Rattanakiri, Uthai Mai Subdistrict,
      Mueang District, Uthai Thani Province\n\u2022 <b>Recitation Master</b>: Phra
      Kru Samuh Pichit Thitaviro, Wat Chantharam, Nam Suem Subdistrict, Mueang District,
      Uthai Thani Province\n\u2022 <b>Instructor</b>: Phra Rajapawanakosol (Anan
      Phutthajano), Wat Chantharam, Nam Suem Subdistrict, Mueang District, Uthai
      Thani Province"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6948907304083456
    m_Localized: Religious Education
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6951314717138944
    m_Localized: "\u2022 <b>Year 2008</b>: Passed the Nak Tham Ek (highest level
      of Dhamma studies) at the Saraburi Province School, Wat Phra Phutthabat Ratchaworamahavihara,
      Phra Phutthabat District, Saraburi Province, certification number S.B. 2351/0078"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6986320470597632
    m_Localized: Secular Education
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6986331421925376
    m_Localized: "\u2022 <b>Year 1961</b>: Graduated from Grade 8, Phibun Witthayalai
      School, Mueang District, Lopburi Province\n\u2022 <b>Year 2010</b>: Honorary
      Master's Degree in Buddhist Studies, specializing in Vipassana Meditation,
      from Mahachulalongkornrajavidyalaya University"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6986434555666432
    m_Localized: Administrative Roles
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6986458626777088
    m_Localized: "\u2022 <b>Year 1995</b>: Acting Abbot of Wat Khao Wong\n\u2022
      <b>Year 1995</b>: Appointed as Abbot of Wat Khao Wong\n\u2022 <b>Year 1995</b>:
      Appointed as Chairman of the Community Training Unit (O.P.T.) in Khao Wong\n\u2022
      <b>Year 2003</b>: Deputy Chief Monk of Phra Phutthabat District\n\u2022 <b>Year
      2009</b>: Appointed Preceptor\n\u2022 <b>Year 2011</b>: Acting Chief Monk of
      Phra Phutthabat District\n\u2022 <b>Year 2012</b>: Appointed Chief Monk of
      Phra Phutthabat District\n\u2022 <b>Year 2014</b>: Participated in the Sangha
      Governance Seminar for Chief Monks and Deputy Chief Monks in Region 1, at the
      Somdej Phra Buddhachinawong Hall, Wat Phitchaya Yatikaram, Khlong San District,
      Bangkok\n\u2022 <b>Year 2015</b>: Appointed Advisor to the Chief Monk of Saraburi
      Province"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6988466859532288
    m_Localized: "\u2022 Morning and evening prayers are performed daily throughout
      the year.\n\n\u2022 Sanghakamma chanting of the Patimokkha is held every half
      month, with at least one monk reciting it.\n\n\u2022 The temple administration
      is conducted according to the Dhamma-Vinaya, the Sangha Act, regulations, announcements,
      orders, and resolutions of the Supreme Sangha Council.\n\n\u2022 Monks, novices,
      and temple students strictly observe temple regulations and other traditions.\n\n\u2022
      Monks, novices, and students are responsible for maintaining temple assets
      and keeping the premises clean and orderly.\n\n\u2022 All monks and novices
      study the Pali Canon according to the Sangha curriculum and attend Dhamma-Vinaya
      training.\n\n\u2022 Monks, novices, or others wishing to stay overnight must
      register for meditation practice with permission from the abbot.\n\n\u2022
      Dhamma study is promoted by supporting monks to attend Nak Tham exams each
      year at the Saraburi Province school.\n\n\u2022 The temple\u2019s rule is that
      monks and novices must perform daily duties, and students are expected to show
      respect for temple governance and the abbot\u2019s instructions.\n\n\u2022
      Conflicts or misunderstandings within the temple are addressed after evening
      prayers daily to prevent serious disputes.\n\n\u2022 Monks, novices, and temple
      attendees are trained to practice meditation as a regular discipline."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
